# V build outputs (root-level binaries only — no trailing slash patterns
# that could swallow source dirs like examples/hello/)
/vails
/vails.exe
/hello
/hello.exe
*.exe
# `v test` leaves the test binary next to its _test.v file; on Linux it has
# no extension (services/dialog_test), on Windows *.exe above covers it.
*_test
.vmodules/
.v/

# The five side-by-side DLLs that `vails build` stages next to the .exe on
# Windows (ADR-0034, B1). They are MSYS2 ucrt64 runtime + webview binaries, not
# source, and they are ~5 MB of the same files in every build directory - so
# without this every `vails build` leaves the tree dirty with binaries that
# belong to the toolchain, not the repo. `*.exe` above already covers the binary
# itself; these are the ones it missed. The app still runs: staging is a copy
# step at build time, and it happens again on the next build.
libwebview-*.dll
WebView2Loader.dll
libgcc_s_seh-*.dll
libstdc++-*.dll
libwinpthread-*.dll

# Docs site build output (`PLAN.md` §7 decision 2: the generated site is an
# artifact, not source. Committed dist/ would mean the generated HTML is reviewed
# as if it were written, which is the "generated file that everyone edits"
# failure `examples/*/frontend/vails.d.ts` already guards against with a header.)
docs/site/dist/

# V's own build scratch for the site binary, which appears next to the source
# rather than in a build directory when the site is compiled in place. The
# directory name carries a timestamp, so it is a fresh one every build and
# `git status` never stops listing it. The lesson it cost: `git add -A` on this
# repository stages ~150 object files from a build nobody meant to commit.
docs/site/.site.exe.*
docs/site/site.exe
